What is the historical context surrounding the founding of the Muramasa school and its significance in Japanese swordsmithing?
The Muramasa school was founded by Muramasa, a famous swordsmith, during the Muromachi period (14th to 16th centuries) in Kuwana, Ise Province, Japan. The exact origin of the school is unknown, but it is believed to have spanned at least three generations. Muramasa's swords were originally renowned for their quality and were favored by the shōgun Tokugawa Ieyasu and his vassals. However, over time, the swords became associated with the anti-Tokugawa movement and were seen as symbols of rebellion. The school's significance in Japanese swordsmithing lies in its contribution to the development of high-quality swords, with some of its students, such as Fujiwara Masazane, creating notable works like the Tonbokiri, one of the Three Great Spears of Japan.
