Evaluate the role of Coughlin's radio show in spreading anti-Semitic sentiment and its consequences.
Charles Coughlin's radio show played a significant role in spreading anti-Semitic sentiment in the United States during the 1930s. His weekly broadcasts, which drew between 5 and 12 million listeners, became a platform for him to express sympathy for fascist policies and to blame the Depression on an international conspiracy of Jewish bankers. He also claimed that Jewish bankers were behind the Russian Revolution, further fueling anti-Semitic themes.

The consequences of Coughlin's radio show were severe. His broadcasts led to the creation of the Christian Front, an organization that attacked Jews in the streets of New York and elsewhere. Coughlin's supporters also organized "Buy Christian" campaigns and attacked Jewish businesses. Additionally, Ivy League universities restricted the numbers of Jews allowed admission, and anti-Semitic sentiment waned only after the Second World War and the Civil Rights Movement.

In summary, Coughlin's radio show was a key factor in spreading anti-Semitic sentiment in the United States during the 1930s, leading to violent attacks on Jews and restrictions on Jewish admission to universities.
